[1mNAME[0m
       fcntl - manipulate file descriptor

[1mLIBRARY[0m
       Standard C library ([4mlibc[24m, [4m-lc[24m)

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
       [1m#include <fcntl.h>[0m

       [1mint fcntl(int [4m[22mfd[24m[1m, int [4m[22mop[24m[1m, ... /* [4m[22marg[24m [1m*/ );[0m

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       [1mfcntl[22m()  performs  one of the operations described below on the open file descriptor [4mfd[24m.  The operation is de‐
       termined by [4mop[24m.

       [1mfcntl[22m() can take an optional third argument.  Whether or not this argument is required is  determined  by  [4mop[24m.
       The required argument type is indicated in parentheses after each [4mop[24m name (in most cases, the required type is
       [4mint[24m, and we identify the argument using the name [4marg[24m), or [4mvoid[24m is specified if the argument is not required.

       Certain  of  the  operations  below are supported only since a particular Linux kernel version.  The preferred
       method of checking whether the host kernel supports a particular operation is to invoke [1mfcntl[22m() with  the  de‐
       sired  [4mop[24m  value and then test whether the call failed with [1mEINVAL[22m, indicating that the kernel does not recog‐
       nize this value.

   [1mDuplicating a file descriptor[0m
       [1mF_DUPFD [22m([4mint[24m)
              Duplicate the file descriptor [4mfd[24m using the lowest-numbered available file descriptor  greater  than  or
              equal to [4marg[24m.  This is different from [1mdup2[22m(2), which uses exactly the file descriptor specified.

              On success, the new file descriptor is returned.

              See [1mdup[22m(2) for further details.

       [1mF_DUPFD_CLOEXEC [22m([4mint[24m; since Linux 2.6.24)
              As  for [1mF_DUPFD[22m, but additionally set the close-on-exec flag for the duplicate file descriptor.  Speci‐
              fying this flag permits a program  to  avoid  an  additional  [1mfcntl[22m()  [1mF_SETFD  [22moperation  to  set  the
              [1mFD_CLOEXEC  [22mflag.   For  an explanation of why this flag is useful, see the description of [1mO_CLOEXEC [22min
              [1mopen[22m(2).

   [1mFile descriptor flags[0m
       The following operations manipulate the flags associated with a file descriptor.   Currently,  only  one  such
       flag  is  defined: [1mFD_CLOEXEC[22m, the close-on-exec flag.  If the [1mFD_CLOEXEC [22mbit is set, the file descriptor will
       automatically be closed during a successful [1mexecve[22m(2).  (If the [1mexecve[22m(2) fails, the file descriptor  is  left
       open.)  If the [1mFD_CLOEXEC [22mbit is not set, the file descriptor will remain open across an [1mexecve[22m(2).

       [1mF_GETFD [22m([4mvoid[24m)
              Return (as the function result) the file descriptor flags; [4marg[24m is ignored.

       [1mF_SETFD [22m([4mint[24m)
              Set the file descriptor flags to the value specified by [4marg[24m.

       In  multithreaded  programs,  using  [1mfcntl[22m() [1mF_SETFD [22mto set the close-on-exec flag at the same time as another
       thread performs a [1mfork[22m(2) plus [1mexecve[22m(2) is vulnerable to a race condition that may unintentionally  leak  the
       file  descriptor  to  the  program executed in the child process.  See the discussion of the [1mO_CLOEXEC [22mflag in
       [1mopen[22m(2) for details and a remedy to the problem.

   [1mFile status flags[0m
       Each open file description has certain associated status flags, initialized by [1mopen[22m(2) and  possibly  modified
       by  [1mfcntl[22m().   Duplicated file descriptors (made with [1mdup[22m(2), [1mfcntl[22m(F_DUPFD), [1mfork[22m(2), etc.) refer to the same
       open file description, and thus share the same file status flags.

       The file status flags and their semantics are described in [1mopen[22m(2).

       [1mF_GETFL [22m([4mvoid[24m)
              Return (as the function result) the file access mode and the file status flags; [4marg[24m is ignored.

       [1mF_SETFL [22m([4mint[24m)
              Set the file status flags to the value specified by [4marg[24m.  File access mode ([1mO_RDONLY[22m, [1mO_WRONLY[22m, [1mO_RDWR[22m)
              and file creation flags (i.e., [1mO_CREAT[22m, [1mO_EXCL[22m, [1mO_NOCTTY[22m, [1mO_TRUNC[22m) in [4marg[24m are ignored.  On Linux,  this
              operation  can change only the [1mO_APPEND[22m, [1mO_ASYNC[22m, [1mO_DIRECT[22m, [1mO_NOATIME[22m, and [1mO_NONBLOCK [22mflags.  It is not
              possible to change the [1mO_DSYNC [22mand [1mO_SYNC [22mflags; see BUGS, below.

   [1mAdvisory record locking[0m
       Linux implements traditional ("process-associated") UNIX record locks, as standardized by POSIX.  For a Linux-
       specific alternative with better semantics, see the discussion of open file description locks below.

       [1mF_SETLK[22m, [1mF_SETLKW[22m, and [1mF_GETLK [22mare used to acquire, release, and test for the existence of record locks  (also
       known  as byte-range, file-segment, or file-region locks).  The third argument, [4mlock[24m, is a pointer to a struc‐
       ture that has at least the following fields (in unspecified order).

           struct flock {
               ...
               short l_type;    /* Type of lock: F_RDLCK,
                                   F_WRLCK, F_UNLCK */
               short l_whence;  /* How to interpret l_start:
                                   SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR, SEEK_END */
               off_t l_start;   /* Starting offset for lock */
               off_t l_len;     /* Number of bytes to lock */
               pid_t l_pid;     /* PID of process blocking our lock
                                   (set by F_GETLK and F_OFD_GETLK) */
               ...
           };

       The [4ml_whence[24m, [4ml_start[24m, and [4ml_len[24m fields of this structure specify the range of bytes we wish to  lock.   Bytes
       past the end of the file may be locked, but not bytes before the start of the file.

       [4ml_start[24m  is the starting offset for the lock, and is interpreted relative to either: the start of the file (if
       [4ml_whence[24m is [1mSEEK_SET[22m); the current file offset (if [4ml_whence[24m is [1mSEEK_CUR[22m); or the end of the file (if  [4ml_whence[0m
       is  [1mSEEK_END[22m).   In the final two cases, [4ml_start[24m can be a negative number provided the offset does not lie be‐
       fore the start of the file.

       [4ml_len[24m specifies the number of bytes to be locked.  If [4ml_len[24m is positive, then the range to  be  locked  covers
       bytes  [4ml_start[24m  up to and including [4ml_start[24m+[4ml_len[24m-1.  Specifying 0 for [4ml_len[24m has the special meaning: lock all
       bytes starting at the location specified by [4ml_whence[24m and [4ml_start[24m through to the end of  file,  no  matter  how
       large the file grows.

       POSIX.1-2001  allows  (but  does not require) an implementation to support a negative [4ml_len[24m value; if [4ml_len[24m is
       negative, the interval described by [4mlock[24m covers bytes [4ml_start[24m+[4ml_len[24m up to and including  [4ml_start[24m-1.   This  is
       supported since Linux 2.4.21 and Linux 2.5.49.

       The  [4ml_type[24m  field  can  be used to place a read ([1mF_RDLCK[22m) or a write ([1mF_WRLCK[22m) lock on a file.  Any number of
       processes may hold a read lock (shared lock) on a file region, but only one process may hold a write lock (ex‐
       clusive lock).  An exclusive lock excludes all other locks, both shared and exclusive.  A single  process  can
       hold  only  one  type of lock on a file region; if a new lock is applied to an already-locked region, then the
       existing lock is converted to the new lock type.  (Such conversions may involve splitting, shrinking, or  coa‐
       lescing with an existing lock if the byte range specified by the new lock does not precisely coincide with the
       range of the existing lock.)

       [1mF_SETLK [22m([4mstruct[24m [4mflock[24m [4m*[24m)
              Acquire  a  lock  (when [4ml_type[24m is [1mF_RDLCK [22mor [1mF_WRLCK[22m) or release a lock (when [4ml_type[24m is [1mF_UNLCK[22m) on the
              bytes specified by the [4ml_whence[24m, [4ml_start[24m, and [4ml_len[24m fields of [4mlock[24m.  If a conflicting lock is  held  by
              another  process, this call returns -1 and sets [4merrno[24m to [1mEACCES [22mor [1mEAGAIN[22m.  (The error returned in this
              case differs across implementations, so POSIX requires a portable application to  check  for  both  er‐
              rors.)

       [1mF_SETLKW [22m([4mstruct[24m [4mflock[24m [4m*[24m)
              As  for [1mF_SETLK[22m, but if a conflicting lock is held on the file, then wait for that lock to be released.
              If a signal is caught while waiting, then the call is interrupted and (after the signal handler has re‐
              turned) returns immediately (with return value -1 and [4merrno[24m set to [1mEINTR[22m; see [1msignal[22m(7)).

       [1mF_GETLK [22m([4mstruct[24m [4mflock[24m [4m*[24m)
              On input to this call, [4mlock[24m describes a lock we would like to place on the file.  If the lock could  be
              placed,  [1mfcntl[22m() does not actually place it, but returns [1mF_UNLCK [22min the [4ml_type[24m field of [4mlock[24m and leaves
              the other fields of the structure unchanged.

              If one or more incompatible locks would prevent this lock being placed, then  [1mfcntl[22m()  returns  details
              about  one of those locks in the [4ml_type[24m, [4ml_whence[24m, [4ml_start[24m, and [4ml_len[24m fields of [4mlock[24m.  If the conflict‐
              ing lock is a traditional (process-associated) record lock, then the [4ml_pid[24m field is set to the  PID  of
              the process holding that lock.  If the conflicting lock is an open file description lock, then [4ml_pid[24m is
              set  to  -1.   Note that the returned information may already be out of date by the time the caller in‐
              spects it.

       In order to place a read lock, [4mfd[24m must be open for reading.  In order to place a write lock, [4mfd[24m must  be  open
       for writing.  To place both types of lock, open a file read-write.

       When  placing locks with [1mF_SETLKW[22m, the kernel detects [4mdeadlocks[24m, whereby two or more processes have their lock
       requests mutually blocked by locks held by the other processes.  For example, suppose process A holds a  write
       lock  on  byte  100 of a file, and process B holds a write lock on byte 200.  If each process then attempts to
       lock the byte already locked by the other process using  [1mF_SETLKW[22m,  then,  without  deadlock  detection,  both
       processes  would  remain  blocked  indefinitely.  When the kernel detects such deadlocks, it causes one of the
       blocking lock requests to immediately fail with the error [1mEDEADLK[22m; an application that encounters such an  er‐
       ror should release some of its locks to allow other applications to proceed before attempting regain the locks
       that  it  requires.   Circular  deadlocks involving more than two processes are also detected.  Note, however,
       that there are limitations to the kernel's deadlock-detection algorithm; see BUGS.

       As well as being removed by an explicit [1mF_UNLCK[22m, record locks are automatically released when the process ter‐
       minates.

       Record locks are not inherited by a child created via [1mfork[22m(2), but are preserved across an [1mexecve[22m(2).

       Because of the buffering performed by the [1mstdio[22m(3) library, the use of record locking with  routines  in  that
       package should be avoided; use [1mread[22m(2) and [1mwrite[22m(2) instead.

       The  record  locks described above are associated with the process (unlike the open file description locks de‐
       scribed below).  This has some unfortunate consequences:

       •  If a process closes [4many[24m file descriptor referring to a file, then all of the process's locks on  that  file
          are released, regardless of the file descriptor(s) on which the locks were obtained.  This is bad: it means
          that a process can lose its locks on a file such as [4m/etc/passwd[24m or [4m/etc/mtab[24m when for some reason a library
          function decides to open, read, and close the same file.

       •  The  threads in a process share locks.  In other words, a multithreaded program can't use record locking to
          ensure that threads don't simultaneously access the same region of a file.

       Open file description locks solve both of these problems.

   [1mOpen file description locks (non-POSIX)[0m
       Open file description locks are advisory byte-range locks whose operation is in most respects identical to the
       traditional record locks described above.  This lock type is Linux-specific, and available since  Linux  3.15.
       (There is a proposal with the Austin Group to include this lock type in the next revision of POSIX.1.)  For an
       explanation of open file descriptions, see [1mopen[22m(2).

       The  principal  difference  between the two lock types is that whereas traditional record locks are associated
       with a process, open file description locks are associated with the open file description on  which  they  are
       acquired,  much  like  locks  acquired  with  [1mflock[22m(2).   Consequently (and unlike traditional advisory record
       locks), open file description locks are inherited across [1mfork[22m(2) (and [1mclone[22m(2) with [1mCLONE_FILES[22m), and are only
       automatically released on the last close of the open file description, instead of being released on any  close
       of the file.

       Conflicting  lock  combinations  (i.e.,  a read lock and a write lock or two write locks) where one lock is an
       open file description lock and the other is a traditional record lock conflict even when they are acquired  by
       the same process on the same file descriptor.

       Open  file description locks placed via the same open file description (i.e., via the same file descriptor, or
       via a duplicate of the file descriptor created by [1mfork[22m(2), [1mdup[22m(2), [1mfcntl[22m() [1mF_DUPFD[22m, and so on) are always com‐
       patible: if a new lock is placed on an already locked region, then the existing lock is converted to  the  new
       lock  type.  (Such conversions may result in splitting, shrinking, or coalescing with an existing lock as dis‐
       cussed above.)

       On the other hand, open file description locks may conflict with each other when they are acquired via differ‐
       ent open file descriptions.  Thus, the threads in a multithreaded program can use open file description  locks
       to  synchronize access to a file region by having each thread perform its own [1mopen[22m(2) on the file and applying
       locks via the resulting file descriptor.

       As with traditional advisory locks, the third argument to [1mfcntl[22m(), [4mlock[24m, is a pointer to an  [4mflock[24m  structure.
       By  contrast  with  traditional record locks, the [4ml_pid[24m field of that structure must be set to zero when using
       the operations described below.

       The operations for working with open file description locks are  analogous  to  those  used  with  traditional
       locks:

       [1mF_OFD_SETLK [22m([4mstruct[24m [4mflock[24m [4m*[24m)
              Acquire  an  open file description lock (when [4ml_type[24m is [1mF_RDLCK [22mor [1mF_WRLCK[22m) or release an open file de‐
              scription lock (when [4ml_type[24m is [1mF_UNLCK[22m) on the bytes specified by  the  [4ml_whence[24m,  [4ml_start[24m,  and  [4ml_len[0m
              fields  of [4mlock[24m.  If a conflicting lock is held by another process, this call returns -1 and sets [4merrno[0m
              to [1mEAGAIN[22m.

       [1mF_OFD_SETLKW [22m([4mstruct[24m [4mflock[24m [4m*[24m)
              As for [1mF_OFD_SETLK[22m, but if a conflicting lock is held on the file, then wait for that lock  to  be  re‐
              leased.   If  a signal is caught while waiting, then the call is interrupted and (after the signal han‐
              dler has returned) returns immediately (with return value -1 and [4merrno[24m set to [1mEINTR[22m; see [1msignal[22m(7)).

       [1mF_OFD_GETLK [22m([4mstruct[24m [4mflock[24m [4m*[24m)
              On input to this call, [4mlock[24m describes an open file description lock we would like to place on the file.
              If the lock could be placed, [1mfcntl[22m() does not actually place it, but  returns  [1mF_UNLCK  [22min  the  [4ml_type[0m
              field  of  [4mlock[24m  and  leaves  the other fields of the structure unchanged.  If one or more incompatible
              locks would prevent this lock being placed, then details about one of  these  locks  are  returned  via
              [4mlock[24m, as described above for [1mF_GETLK[22m.

       In the current implementation, no deadlock detection is performed for open file description locks.  (This con‐
       trasts with process-associated record locks, for which the kernel does perform deadlock detection.)

   [1mMandatory locking[0m
       [4mWarning[24m: the Linux implementation of mandatory locking is unreliable.  See BUGS below.  Because of these bugs,
       and  the fact that the feature is believed to be little used, since Linux 4.5, mandatory locking has been made
       an optional feature, governed by a configuration option ([1mCONFIG_MANDATORY_FILE_LOCKING[22m).  This feature  is  no
       longer supported at all in Linux 5.15 and above.

       By  default, both traditional (process-associated) and open file description record locks are advisory.  Advi‐
       sory locks are not enforced and are useful only between cooperating processes.

       Both lock types can also be mandatory.  Mandatory locks are enforced for all processes.  If a process tries to
       perform an incompatible access (e.g., [1mread[22m(2) or [1mwrite[22m(2)) on a file region that has an incompatible mandatory
       lock, then the result depends upon whether the [1mO_NONBLOCK [22mflag is enabled for its open file  description.   If
       the  [1mO_NONBLOCK [22mflag is not enabled, then the system call is blocked until the lock is removed or converted to
       a mode that is compatible with the access.  If the [1mO_NONBLOCK [22mflag is enabled, then the system call fails with
       the error [1mEAGAIN[22m.

       To make use of mandatory locks, mandatory locking must be enabled both on the  filesystem  that  contains  the
       file  to  be locked, and on the file itself.  Mandatory locking is enabled on a filesystem using the "-o mand"
       option to [1mmount[22m(8), or the [1mMS_MANDLOCK [22mflag for [1mmount[22m(2).  Mandatory locking is enabled on a file by disabling
       group execute permission on the file and enabling the set-group-ID permission bit (see [1mchmod[22m(1) and [1mchmod[22m(2)).

       Mandatory locking is not specified by POSIX.  Some other systems also support mandatory locking, although  the
       details of how to enable it vary across systems.

   [1mLost locks[0m
       When an advisory lock is obtained on a networked filesystem such as NFS it is possible that the lock might get
       lost.   This  may happen due to administrative action on the server, or due to a network partition (i.e., loss
       of network connectivity with the server) which lasts long enough for the server to assume that the  client  is
       no longer functioning.

       When  the  filesystem  determines that a lock has been lost, future [1mread[22m(2) or [1mwrite[22m(2) requests may fail with
       the error [1mEIO[22m.  This error will persist until the lock is removed or the file  descriptor  is  closed.   Since
       Linux 3.12, this happens at least for NFSv4 (including all minor versions).

       Some  versions  of  UNIX send a signal ([1mSIGLOST[22m) in this circumstance.  Linux does not define this signal, and
       does not provide any asynchronous notification of lost locks.

