   [1mF_GETOWN[0m
       A  limitation of the Linux system call conventions on some architectures (notably i386) means that if a (nega‐
       tive) process group ID to be returned by [1mF_GETOWN [22mfalls in the range -1 to -4095, then  the  return  value  is
       wrongly  interpreted by glibc as an error in the system call; that is, the return value of [1mfcntl[22m() will be -1,
       and [4merrno[24m will contain the (positive) process group ID.  The Linux-specific [1mF_GETOWN_EX [22moperation avoids  this
       problem.   Since  glibc 2.11, glibc makes the kernel [1mF_GETOWN [22mproblem invisible by implementing [1mF_GETOWN [22musing
       [1mF_GETOWN_EX[22m.

   [1mF_SETOWN[0m
       In Linux 2.4 and earlier, there is bug that can occur when an unprivileged process uses  [1mF_SETOWN  [22mto  specify
       the  owner  of a socket file descriptor as a process (group) other than the caller.  In this case, [1mfcntl[22m() can
       return -1 with [4merrno[24m set to [1mEPERM[22m, even when the owner process (group) is one that the caller  has  permission
       to  send signals to.  Despite this error return, the file descriptor owner is set, and signals will be sent to
       the owner.

   [1mDeadlock detection[0m
       The deadlock-detection algorithm employed by the kernel when dealing with [1mF_SETLKW  [22mrequests  can  yield  both
       false negatives (failures to detect deadlocks, leaving a set of deadlocked processes blocked indefinitely) and
       false  positives ([1mEDEADLK [22merrors when there is no deadlock).  For example, the kernel limits the lock depth of
       its dependency search to 10 steps, meaning that circular deadlock chains that exceed that size will not be de‐
       tected.  In addition, the kernel may falsely indicate a deadlock when two or more processes created using  the
       [1mclone[22m(2) [1mCLONE_FILES [22mflag place locks that appear (to the kernel) to conflict.

   [1mMandatory locking[0m
       The  Linux  implementation  of  mandatory  locking is subject to race conditions which render it unreliable: a
       [1mwrite[22m(2) call that overlaps with a lock may modify data after the mandatory lock is acquired; a  [1mread[22m(2)  call
       that  overlaps  with  a  lock  may detect changes to data that were made only after a write lock was acquired.
       Similar races exist between mandatory locks and [1mmmap[22m(2).  It is therefore inadvisable  to  rely  on  mandatory
       locking.

